# Market failure or government failure — which is worse?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "One side: markets fail systematically, the state must act The other: the state fails too, and often worse"
next: {"title":"What do economists actually agree on about markets?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/68-what-do-economists-actually-agree-on.cast"}

## One law, two verdicts
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Costs
      - Benefits
    values:
      - 0.5
      - 22.2
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 30
    y_label: $ trillion
    colors:
      - red
      - green
    gap: 0.6
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 110
      w: 470
      h: 370
sources:
    - id: metoffice1952
      title: The Great Smog of 1952
      authors: Met Office
      year: 2022
      finding: Smog over London 5–9 December 1952; about 4,000 excess deaths in the official count at the time, later estimates around 10,000–12,000. Led to the Clean Air Act 1956.
      url: https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/case-studies/great-smog
    - id: epa1997
      title: The Benefits and Costs of the Clean Air Act, 1970 to 1990
      authors: U.S. Environmental Protection Agency
      year: 1997
      finding: Direct compliance costs about $523 billion; central benefit estimate $22.2 trillion (range $5.6–49.4 trillion), 1990 dollars; benefits mostly avoided premature deaths and illness.
    - id: winston2006
      title: 'Government Failure versus Market Failure: Microeconomics Policy Research and Government Performance'
      authors: Clifford Winston
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Reviewing scholarly assessments of US policies: for pollution command-and-control regulation ''the most reasonable point estimate suggest[s] small net benefits'', as large benefits were offset by high costs (Portney 1990; Hazilla and Kopp 1990). AEI-Brookings Joint Center.'

    box tag_a "A: the state must act" #2f6b8f x 260 y 585 width 400 height 66 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box tag_b "B: the cure is often worse" #b5482e x 745 y 585 width 440 height 66 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    icon smog_icon size 150 x 500 y 420 icon_look picture of factory hidden true
    text smog_where "London, Dec 1952" #3d3833 x 500 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    text smog_deaths "4,000 deaths" #b5482e cites ["metoffice1952"] x 500 y 225 font_size 36 hidden true
    text smog_later "later: ~12,000" #b5482e cites ["metoffice1952"] x 500 y 160 font_size 34 hidden true
    text chart_t "Clean Air Act\n1970–90, EPA\n(1990 dollars)" #3d3833 cites ["epa1997"] x 800 y 495 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ratio "42 to 1" #4f8a3a cites ["epa1997"] x 800 y 400 font_size 48 hidden true
    text range "range 5.6 – 49.4" #8f887c cites ["epa1997"] x 800 y 338 font_size 30 hidden true
    box winston "Winston (2006):\nsmall net benefits" #b5482e cites ["winston2006"] x 800 y 245 width 300 height 120 radius 12 font_size 28 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text who "Who counts?" #b5482e x 800 y 145 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Market failure or government failure?"

Which does more harm: markets that fail, or governments th (@draw tag_a@)at try to fix them? We're taking opposite sides on this one. My side: when markets  (@highlight tag_a@)fail, the state must act.

B: And mine (@draw tag_b@): the state fails too, and often worse than the market it's fixing.

Then I'll op (@draw smog_icon@)en with London, December 1952. Smoke from coal f (@draw smog_where@)ires in homes and factories sat over the city for five days.

B: The government's coun (@draw smog_deaths@)t was about four thousand extra deaths. Later estima (@draw smog_later@)tes say around twelve thousand.

And nobody burning coal paid a penny for it: the cost fell outside eve (@erase smog_icon smog_where smog_deaths smog_later@)ry deal. Only a law could fix that. Britain passed a Clean Air Act in 1956; America a bi (@draw chart_t ends@)gger one in 1970.

    draw axes

B: Its agency, the EPA, added up the first twenty years. Cost (@draw bar_1@)s: about half a trillion dollars, in 1990 money.

    ask "Drag the Benefits bar to your guess, in trillions." on bar_2 store g default "3"
        intro: And the benefits, mostly lives and health?
        right: Close: you said {g} trillion; the EPA's estimate was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} trillion; the EPA's estimate was {g.true}.

Twenty-two trillion. About forty- (@draw ratio@)two dollars back for every dollar spent.

B: By the agency's own count. And its range ra (@draw range@)n all the way from about six trillion to forty-nine.

Even the bottom of that range is ten times the cost.
    highlight range bar_1

B: Now read the independent studies. Clifford Winston reviewed (@draw winston@) them in 2006, and for command rules like these, the most reasonable estimate he foun (@highlight winston@)d was small net benefits.

One law, two verdicts. Before we can say which failure is worse, we have t (@draw who@)o ask who's doing the counting.
    highlight ratio winston

    pause 0.5

## Winston's ledger
sources:
    - id: winston2006
      title: 'Government Failure versus Market Failure: Microeconomics Policy Research and Government Performance'
      authors: Clifford Winston
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Review of scholarly assessments of US federal policies against market failures: ''I report success stories, but few of them emerged from my search.'' Lead-permit trading (Hahn and Hester 1989) and SO2 allowance trading were cost-effective successes; command-and-control emission rules were ''expensive successes'' (new-car emission technology raised a new car''s cost nearly $1,000 in the mid-1980s, Crandall et al. 1986), and taxes or tradable permits could have cut emissions at much lower cost; airline and other economic deregulation from the late 1970s brought large consumer gains.'
    - id: gao2000
      title: 'Sugar Program: Supporting Sugar Prices Has Increased Users'' Costs While Benefiting Producers (GAO/RCED-00-126)'
      authors: U.S. General Accounting Office
      year: 2000
      finding: The sugar program cost domestic sweetener users about $1.9 billion in 1998; sugar beet and cane producers gained about $1 billion.
      url: https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-00-126.pdf
    - id: davis2008
      title: The Effect of Driving Restrictions on Air Quality in Mexico City
      authors: Lucas W. Davis
      year: 2008
      finding: 'No evidence that Hoy No Circula improved air quality, across pollutants. Journal of Political Economy 116(1): 38–81.'

    box tag_a "A: the state must act" #2f6b8f x 250 y 610 width 360 height 56 radius 12 font_size 24 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box tag_b "B: the cure is often worse" #b5482e x 750 y 610 width 400 height 56 radius 12 font_size 24 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    box book "Winston (2006): US policies\nagainst market failures" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 500 y 420 width 520 height 110 radius 12 icon book icon_side left font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    text quote "“…but few of them emerged\nfrom my search.”" #b5482e cites ["winston2006"] x 500 y 270 font_size 34 hidden true
    box sugar0 "Sugar price supports" #b5482e x 500 y 430 width 420 height 90 radius 12 icon sugar icon_side left font_size 32 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text sug_users "$1.9bn from users" #b5482e cites ["gao2000"] x 500 y 310 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sug_grow "→ $1bn to growers (1998)" #3d3833 cites ["gao2000"] x 500 y 240 font_size 34 hidden true
    cards sortq check each hidden true
        Cheap fix: Lead permits
        Costly fix: Car rules
        No gain or worse: Airline fares
        Cheap fix: Acid-rain permits
        No gain or worse: Mexico car ban
    text h1 "Cheap fix" #4f8a3a x 180 y 540 font_size 32 hidden true
    text h2 "Costly fix" #c47a2c x 500 y 540 font_size 32 hidden true
    text h3 "No gain or worse" #b5482e x 820 y 540 font_size 32 hidden true
    box lead "Lead-in-petrol permits" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 180 y 455 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    box acid "Acid-rain permits" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 180 y 360 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    box cars "Car emission rules" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 500 y 455 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    text cars_cost "+ ~$1,000 a car" #c47a2c cites ["winston2006"] x 500 y 370 font_size 32 hidden true
    box sugar "Sugar price supports" #3d3833 cites ["gao2000"] x 820 y 455 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    box air "Airline fares (to 1978)" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 820 y 360 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    box mex "Mexico City driving ban" #3d3833 cites ["davis2008"] x 820 y 265 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    box priced "Cars: priced instead?" dashed #8f887c x 180 y 265 width 300 height 84 radius 12 font_size 30 style.stroke #8f887c hidden true
    arrow swap dashed #8f887c from.anchor left from.ref cars to.anchor top_right to.ref priced hidden true
    text c_price "Price it" #4f8a3a x 180 y 180 font_size 34 hidden true
    text c_presc "Prescribe it" #c47a2c x 500 y 265 font_size 34 hidden true
    text c_know "Knowledge" #c47a2c x 500 y 265 font_size 34 hidden true
    text c_inc "Incentives" #b5482e x 820 y 180 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Winston's ledger"

    draw tag_a tag_b parallel true

B: So let's find a count that isn't the agency marking its own ho (@draw book@)mework. Winston gathered decades of studies on American policies meant to fix market failures.

How many successes did he find?
    highlight book

B: His own words: he reported suc (@draw quote@)cess stories, 'but few of them emerged from my search.'

B: Worse, the state often steps in where no market has failed at all. Take sugar: in (@draw sugar0@) 1998, America's price supports cost the people who buy sugar a (@draw sug_users@)bout one point nine billion dollars.
    erase book quote

To han (@draw sug_grow@)d the growers about one. A few who gain a lot, against mi (@highlight sug_grow@)llions who each lose a little and never notice: the lobby wins.

    erase sugar0 sug_users sug_grow

    erase tag_a tag_b

    draw sortq

    ask "Did each fix its problem cheaply, at a high price, or achieve nothing, or worse? Sort the cards." on sortq store srt stage own voice b
        intro: Now sort these five real policies.
        right: All five right: the permits were the cheap fixes, the car rules the costly one, and the fare rules and the Mexico ban gained nothing.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try. The grouping: the two permit schemes were cheap fixes, the car rules a costly one, and the airline fares and the Mexico ban no gain.

    erase sortq

    draw tag_a tag_b parallel true

The two cheap (@draw h1 lead acid@) fixes are the ones that put a price on pollution: tradable permit (@highlight lead acid@)s to phase lead out of petrol, and to cut acid rain.

B: The rules that t (@draw h2 cars@)old carmakers exactly what to fit did clean the air, but by the mid-1980s (@draw cars_cost@) they added nearly a thousand dollars to a new car. An expensive success.

And the (@draw h3 sugar@) failures: sugar, the airline far (@draw air@)es the state fixed until 1978, and Mexico City (@draw mex@)'s driving ban, which left the air no cleaner.

    erase cars_cost

B: Now change one thing. S (@draw swap priced@)wap the car rules' command for a price on emi (@draw c_price@)ssions, and Winston's claim is that th (@draw c_presc@)e same clean air would have come far cheaper.

    erase swap priced c_price c_presc

So the state fails a lot. But it fails in particular ways: when it gue (@highlight cars@)sses what it  (@draw c_know@)can't know, like which technology every firm should fit.

B: Or when the p (@highlight sugar air@)eople who gain from a bad rule are the ones writing it. Knowledge and incentives:  (@draw c_inc@)the two ways a state goes wrong.

    pause 0.5

## Which is worse?
sources:
    - id: winston2006
      title: 'Government Failure versus Market Failure: Microeconomics Policy Research and Government Performance'
      authors: Clifford Winston
      year: 2006
      finding: Draws on scholarly studies and 'for the most part' not on studies by government agencies; notes 'there may be a fundamental bias in published economics research that favors negative conclusions about government performance' (footnote 3). Recommends market-oriented tools (pollution taxes, tradable permits) over command-and-control.

    box claimA "A: Markets fail worse" #2f6b8f x 255 y 575 width 400 height 74 radius 12 font_size 32 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box claimB "B: States fail worse" #b5482e x 745 y 575 width 400 height 74 radius 12 font_size 32 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text bs_h "The ledger's blind spots" #8f887c x 500 y 480 font_size 34 hidden true
    text bs1 "• Unfixed failures aren't scored (London 1952)" #3d3833 x 500 y 405 font_size 34 hidden true
    text bs2 "• Agencies' own studies left out" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 500 y 335 font_size 34 hidden true
    text bs3 "• Critics like finding fault" #3d3833 cites ["winston2006"] x 500 y 265 font_size 34 hidden true
    mark strikeA around claimA kind strike hidden true
    mark strikeB around claimB kind strike hidden true
    box well "State does well:\nbig, measurable harm\n+ a price on it" #4f8a3a x 255 y 450 width 430 height 175 radius 12 font_size 34 style.stroke #4f8a3a hidden true
    box badly "State does badly:\nguesswork, or a lobby\nholding the pen" #b5482e x 745 y 450 width 430 height 175 radius 12 font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text s1 "Both fail, often: consensus" #3d3833 x 500 y 315 font_size 34 hidden true
    text s2 "Price beats prescribe: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 260 font_size 34 hidden true
    text s3 "Which is worse overall: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 205 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Which is worse?"

    draw claimA claimB parallel true

Winston's ledger hurts my side. But it has a (@draw bs_h@) blind spot: it scores pol (@draw bs1@)icies, and a failure nobody tried to fix is not a policy.

B: Like London's smoke before anyone passed a law.
    highlight bs1

Right. And he left ou (@draw bs2@)t the agencies' own studies, so the forty-two to on (@highlight bs2 effect underline@)e never made it into his count.

B: Fair. And Winston admits acade (@draw bs3@)mics tend to be critics, so published research may lea (@highlight bs3 effect underline@)n toward bad news about governments.

Then neither blanket claim survi (@draw strikeA@)ves: not 'markets fail worse', and n (@draw strikeB@)ot 'states fail worse'.

    erase bs_h bs1 bs2 bs3

B: What survives is a pattern. The s (@draw well@)tate did well where the harm was big and measurable, and (@highlight well effect box@) it put a price on it.

And badly (@draw badly@) where it had to guess what nobody knew, or where a  (@highlight badly effect box@)lobby was holding the pen.

B: How sure are we? That mark (@draw s1@)ets and states both fail, often and expensively: consensus.

That pricing a h (@draw s2@)arm beats prescribing the fix:  (@highlight well@)mostly agreed among economists.

B: Which f (@draw s3@)ailure is worse overall: contested, and may (@highlight s3 effect underline@)be the wrong question.

    quiz "A city wants less rubbish sent to landfill. Plan 1: a fee for every tonne dumped, passed on to households per bin collected. Plan 2: a rule on which recycling bins every household must buy. Which looks more like the cheap fixes?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The fee per tonne. Like the permits, it prices the harm and lets each household and hauler find the cheapest way to cut it.
        wrong: Of the cheap fixes on the ledger, did any tell people exactly what to buy? None did: here, the fee per tonne.
        + The fee per tonne
        * The bins rule
        * They should cost the same

    quiz "Rice farmers ask for a tariff on imported rice 'for food security'. Which failure should worry you most?" voice b
        right: Government failure. The gain is concentrated on a few farmers, the cost spread thin over every rice buyer, the sugar pattern.
        wrong: Count who gains, and how much each, against who pays: a few farmers gain a lot, many pay a little. Here, government failure.
        + Government failure: a few gain a lot, many pay a little
        * Market failure: rice is a public good
        * Neither: tariffs cost nothing

    quiz "A study says a new safety rule returned ten dollars for every dollar it cost. The agency that wrote the rule did the study. What's the best next step?"
        right: Look for an independent study. One law can get two verdicts; the agency's count may be right, but check it.
        wrong: Think of the one law with two verdicts: here, look for an independent study.
        * Accept it: ten to one is huge
        + Look for an independent study of the same rule
        * Assume the rule failed

So which is worse? Neither, as a rule. Real markets fail and real states fail; the question worth asking is which fix, counted by whom, and written by whom.
    highlight well badly

    pause 0.5

