      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:8km79b'
    '|a||Three new cases. A city wants less rubbish sent to landfill. Plan 1: a fee for every tonne dumped, passed on to households per bin collected. Plan 2: a rule on which recycling bins every household must buy. Which looks more like the cheap fixes?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 16880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:vjzheu'
    '|a||The fee per tonne. Like the permits, it prices the harm and lets each household and hauler find the cheapest way to cut it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7880
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Rice farmers ask for a tariff on imported rice ''for food security''. Which failure should worry you most?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Government failure. The gain is concentrated on a few farmers, the cost spread thin over every rice buyer, the sugar pattern.':
